R(296,"BD","secure","Encryption at rest","Use EBS encryption and S3 server-side encryption for stored data; TLS protects a different stage of the data lifecycle.",
"TLS protects network transport, not the stored EBS blocks.|EBS encryption protects volume data and associated snapshots at rest using KMS keys.|HTTPS protects S3 requests in transit, not the explicit at-rest key configuration.|S3 server-side encryption protects stored objects; configure SSE-KMS when centralized customer key control is required.","ebs sse", "For guardrails, enable EBS encryption by default per Region and set the intended S3 default/bucket-policy controls. Defaults do not retroactively encrypt all existing data.")
R(297,"BC","secure","Hybrid connectivity","A standard VPC Site-to-Site VPN needs a customer gateway with reachable addressing and an attached virtual private gateway.",
"A NAT instance in the VPC route table is not a prerequisite for the managed VPN attachment.|The internet VPN needs a reachable customer gateway address; supported NAT traversal uses the public address of the fronting NAT device.|A VGW attached to the VPC supplies the AWS-side VPN termination when Transit Gateway is not used.|AWS manages VPN endpoint addresses; customers do not associate an Elastic IP with a VGW.","vpn")
h(298,"The instance EBS bandwidth ceiling is the stated bottleneck. A larger suitable instance can raise it, but switching to provisioned-IOPS volumes alone cannot exceed that same instance ceiling. Without an additional volume IOPS constraint, the source does not justify its required TWO answers.","performance","Block storage performance","ebs")
R(299,"B","resilient","Database backups","Setting automated-backup retention to zero disables RDS automated backups and their point-in-time recovery capability.",
"Removing automated backups sacrifices recovery points and transaction-log recovery, not just storage charges.|Without automated backups, the managed snapshot/log chain needed for point-in-time recovery is no longer maintained.|Manual snapshots remain possible but restore to their captured state, not arbitrary intervening times.|Supported RDS DB instances can disable automated backups with retention zero; deployment-specific restrictions still apply.","pit")
R(300,"A","secure","Directory federation","AD Connector proxies authentication to an existing on-premises AD instead of replicating directory data into AWS.",
"AD Connector forwards supported directory requests to the existing AD and avoids a separate replicated cloud directory.|Simple AD creates a separate directory; it is not a proxy for the existing corporate directory.|New EC2 domain controllers introduce the cloud replication and host administration the question excludes.|There is no IAM VPC endpoint that turns RDP into corporate AD authentication.","ad", "Clients must still be correctly domain joined and privately connected to the required directory/DNS services; AD Connector is not a replacement for that networking.")
R(301,"B","performance","Database selection","Small keyed preference documents and high-throughput millisecond access fit DynamoDB's key-value/document model.",
"RDS supports relational queries but introduces a different scaling/relational model from the simple internet-scale keyed access required.|DynamoDB provides managed horizontal scale and low-latency keyed reads/writes for items below its size limit.|Glacier is archival object storage, not an interactive preference database.|Redshift is an analytical warehouse rather than the operational low-latency preference store.","dynamo")
R(302,"D","performance","Content delivery","CloudFront provides global edge delivery in front of an S3 origin; private origin access and failover require explicit configuration.",
"RDS read replicas do not replicate S3 static objects or create an edge CDN.|DNS routing alone does not cache assets at edge locations or authorize private origin access.|NLB is not a static-object cache and cannot use an S3 website endpoint as a normal registered load-balancer target.|CloudFront caches near viewers and supports origin access controls, viewer controls and configured origin failover.","cloudfront oac", "Keep the S3 REST origin private with OAC; configure an origin group/secondary origin if failover is needed. A distribution alone does not create a backup origin.")
R(303,"B","secure","Private service access","S3 gateway endpoints are VPC-local; use an endpoint-scoped bucket-policy restriction rather than relying on an internet route or peering transit.",
"NAT reaches public service endpoints and does not enforce the requested endpoint-only access path.|A gateway endpoint in VPC-A provides private S3 routing; an appropriate aws:SourceVpce deny/exception policy can enforce the approved path.|A peer cannot transit VPC-B's S3 gateway endpoint; the endpoint belongs to the accessing VPC.|S3 is not placed behind a customer NLB to implement this gateway-endpoint policy pattern.","s3endpoint", "An allow statement alone does not override other allows. Use a carefully scoped explicit deny for non-approved paths, including needed AWS-service/admin exceptions.")
R(304,"C","performance","Batch computing","Batch combines job queues, dependencies/retries and managed scalable compute for long-running batch work.",
"EC2 plus cron leaves scheduling, queueing, retries and fleet integration to custom operations.|An individual Lambda invocation has a bounded execution duration and is not the fit for arbitrarily long batch jobs.|AWS Batch offers managed scheduling and compute environments, including appropriate Spot capacity choices.|Scheduled ECS services can run containers but do not alone provide the complete Batch job-queue and scheduling feature set.","batch")
R(305,"D","secure","Network access controls","Security groups allow the intended inbound application ports and implicitly deny unmatched ingress.",
"Outbound rules govern instance-initiated egress, not internet clients opening HTTP/HTTPS connections.|A subnet NACL is not a replacement for correct instance security-group ingress and affects every resource in the subnet.|Allowing global SSH violates the explicit HTTP/HTTPS-only requirement.|Inbound TCP 80/443 rules allow the web protocols; remove other ingress rules and check every attached SG.","vpc")
R(306,"B","resilient","Messaging & integration","SQS decouples producers from database workers, buffering bursts with visibility timeouts, retries and dead-letter handling.",
"Cooldown settings change scaling reactions but do not durably buffer HTTP-originated work.|SQS retains work independently of producers/workers, allowing controlled consumption and per-message failure handling.|An ALB is not a durable message queue and cannot buffer transactional database writes this way.|Read caching does not decouple or durably buffer a burst of pending database writes.","sqs", "Worker concurrency must be limited to downstream database capacity. Use idempotency because delivery/retries can repeat work.")
h(307,"Aurora MySQL's Lambda integration can invoke Lambda from database logic, but merely publishing unrelated messages to SQS does not capture Aurora row changes. Option C omits the change-to-queue producer, so exactly TWO complete solutions are not supplied.","resilient","Database change events","dynamostream lambda")
R(308,"BD","secure","Database encryption","Enable the RDS-managed encryption feature and select the intended KMS key; customers do not administer its underlying EBS volumes.",
"The RDS service owns underlying storage configuration; manually encrypting a customer EC2 EBS volume is not the RDS control.|RDS encryption protects managed database storage, logs, backups and supported replicas through the service feature.|S3 encryption does not encrypt the running RDS database storage.|KMS supplies the centrally controlled key used by the RDS encryption feature and its authorization policy.","rdsencrypt kmspolicy")
R(309,"A","performance","Big data processing","EMR supplies the Spark processing environment while S3 decouples durable data from transient compute.",
"EMR manages the Spark platform and S3 provides elastic durable storage independent of worker lifetimes.|On-premises Spark retains the cluster investment/operations the migration is intended to avoid.|Instance store is temporary worker storage and is not the durable data-lake copy for petabyte datasets.|This retains on-premises compute and relies on instance-local temporary storage rather than managed elastic lake storage.","emr s3")
R(310,"D","performance","Database capacity","DynamoDB auto scaling adjusts provisioned capacity within configured bounds; it is reactive and does not guarantee zero throttling during abrupt spikes.",
"A load balancer does not raise a DynamoDB table's provisioned read/write capacity.|A fixed read-capacity value cannot adapt to future changing demand and may target the wrong operation.|Read/write capacity units serve different operations; increasing one does not substitute for the other.|Auto scaling adjusts the relevant provisioned capacities within minimum/maximum limits, fitting the required capacity mode.","dynamoauto", "The source overstates an immediate permanent fix. Scaling has detection/update delays; preserve headroom, retry with backoff and configure indexes independently. A capacity maximum is not an absolute total-bill ceiling.")
R(311,"C","secure","Secret rotation","Secrets Manager provides encrypted secret storage, scoped retrieval, auditability and supported rotation workflows.",
"Hard-coded repository credentials expose long-lived secrets and do not implement managed rotation.|Changing function language does not store, protect or rotate a database password.|Secrets Manager supports KMS protection, IAM-authorized retrieval and configured database credential rotation.|Tags are resource labels, not an encrypted secret store or a credential rotation system.","secrets", "Configure a supported rotation strategy, connectivity and application refresh/caching behavior. Merely storing the password does not enable rotation or guarantee zero downtime.")
R(312,"B","performance","Streaming ingestion","Firehose can buffer/compress and deliver telemetry to S3 and supported Redshift loading workflows without custom stream-consumer hosts.",
"Kinesis Streams plus EC2 staging requires custom consumers and instance-local data handling.|Firehose's managed delivery and S3 staging fit a durable lake and supported Redshift COPY-based ingestion.|EC2 is not the ordinary Firehose managed delivery destination implied here and adds unnecessary storage hosts.|Streams can support this with custom consumers, but the choice omits that processing/delivery implementation and adds operations.","firehose redshift")
R(313,"C","cost","Compute scaling","Scheduled scaling raises capacity before a recurring known demand spike rather than waiting for utilization alarms.",
"Simple scaling is reactive to an alarm and can lag while instances bootstrap.|Step scaling also responds to observed breaches rather than anticipating the known clock-time spike.|Scheduled actions can prepare desired/minimum capacity before 7 AM and reduce it after the rush.|Keeping a static fleet cannot both prepare for spikes and efficiently reduce off-peak capacity.","scheduled")
R(314,"A","secure","Database encryption","Redshift can enable KMS encryption on an existing cluster; the service migrates it to encrypted storage during the modify operation.",
"Modify the cluster's encryption configuration to use the chosen KMS key; Redshift manages the migration.|Customers do not directly reconfigure Redshift's underlying EBS volumes to implement cluster encryption.|TLS/SSL protects client transport, not database blocks at rest.|A manually created cluster/data migration is not required for supported KMS encryption changes, although other encryption types can need a different process.","redshiftencrypt", "Plan the documented read-only/resizing interval and snapshot-copy prerequisites. Enabling encryption is not a zero-impact switch.")
h(315,"EKS on Fargate satisfies no worker-node management and per-pod isolation, but the stated EBS CSI requirement is unsupported: Fargate pods cannot mount EBS volumes. Fargate also has specific Pod Identity/agent restrictions. No choice satisfies every listed requirement.","performance","Container compute","eksfargate")
R(316,"DE","performance","AI service selection","Transcribe converts speech to text; Translate converts that resulting text into another language.",
"Kendra is enterprise search, not the requested speech recognition and text translation pipeline.|Rekognition analyzes images/video, not general podcast transcription.|Lex provides conversational interfaces rather than bulk podcast-to-multilingual-document conversion.|Transcribe recognizes speech in the audio and outputs a transcript.|Translate accepts the transcript text and produces supported target-language versions.","transcribe translate")
