R(318,"A","secure","Content moderation","Rekognition moderation APIs provide pretrained image/video content-category detection with confidence values.",
"Rekognition supplies the managed visual moderation APIs described, including supported image and video workflows.|Comprehend analyzes text rather than detecting visual content in product photos.|SageMaker can host/train custom models but adds model/inference engineering compared with the requested pretrained API.|Kendra indexes/searches enterprise information and is not a visual moderation model.","reko", "Use confidence thresholds and review workflows appropriate to the policy. The application must take the block/review action; model labels alone are not a policy enforcement guarantee.")
R(319,"D","cost","TLS & certificates","ALB HTTPS listener certificate lists support SNI so independent domains can use independent certificates on one listener.",
"Another ALB adds load-balancer cost unnecessarily when certificate selection is supported on the existing listener.|One wildcard under one domain cannot cover two unrelated registrable domains.|A SAN certificate can cover both but requires certificate reissuance when its name list changes, contrary to the stated preference.|Add both certificates to the listener; SNI-capable clients allow the ALB to choose an appropriate matching certificate.","sni")
R(320,"D","resilient","DNS & routing","Weighted Route 53 records express a deliberate proportional DNS traffic split between regional endpoints.",
"Simple routing does not express the desired distinct 50/50 or rollout weights.|Failover selects a primary or secondary, not an intentional active-active proportion.|Latency routing chooses based on latency rather than an explicitly configured traffic percentage.|Weighted routing assigns relative weights to endpoint records, supporting phased rollouts and active-active splits.","routing", "Weights govern DNS-answer proportions over queries; caching/client behavior means actual HTTP-request counts need not be exactly 50/50.")
R(321,"D","cost","Object storage lifecycle","An AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload lifecycle rule removes abandoned uploaded parts after a configured age.",
"Compression may reduce upload size but does not clean up already abandoned multipart parts.|Moving completed videos to archive classes does not abort incomplete uploads.|Incomplete upload parts are not transitioned to Glacier by an object storage-class transition rule.|The lifecycle abort-incomplete action removes the stale multipart upload parts and stops their ongoing storage charges.","lifecycle", "The action is specifically aborting incomplete multipart uploads, not ordinary object expiration; its threshold is age since initiation.")
R(322,"A","secure","Private content delivery","Use OAC with an S3 REST origin and scoped bucket/KMS policies to support private SSE-KMS content delivery.",
"OAC authorizes the CloudFront service principal for the intended distribution; keep Block Public Access and grant required KMS access separately.|An S3 website endpoint is a custom origin and does not support the claimed OAI integration.|A public bucket allows viewers to bypass CloudFront signed-viewer controls by fetching directly.|IP allowlisting is broad/fragile and does not establish distribution-scoped origin authorization or KMS use.","oac kmspolicy", "The bucket policy grants the CloudFront service principal with a distribution SourceArn condition; an OAC is not itself a bucket-policy principal.")
R(323,"B","cost","Compute purchasing","Use non-interruptible purchasing for workloads that cannot tolerate interruption and Spot for recoverable non-critical tasks.",
"This places critical services on interruptible capacity and reverses the required reliability tradeoff.|On-Demand supplies the stable critical baseline while Spot discounts restartable/batch work.|All-Spot exposes critical services to interruption contrary to the explicit requirement.|All-On-Demand meets interruption needs but misses savings on the specified interruption-tolerant tasks.","spot fargate", "Use separate capacity providers/strategies, graceful interruption handling and checkpoints. On-Demand is not a guarantee against instance failures; HA design is still required.")
R(324,"C","secure","Browser API access","CORS tells browsers which cross-origin requests/headers are permitted; it is separate from authentication and authorization.",
"Stages identify API deployment environments, not cross-origin browser permission rules.|Deployments publish an API configuration but do not inherently allow a browser origin.|Configure appropriate CORS responses, allowed origins/methods/headers and preflight handling for the SPA.|A generic API Access label is not the browser cross-origin feature required.","cors", "Restrict allowed origins deliberately and retain real API authorization. CORS is enforced by browsers and is not a general API security boundary.")
R(325,"B","performance","Compute scaling","For simple scaling policies, cooldown avoids repeating adjustments before recent changes have taken effect; other policy types use warm-up differently.",
"A user-data sleep delays the instance rather than coordinating the Auto Scaling policy's evaluation behavior.|The cooldown interval is the listed control for waiting between applicable simple-scaling actions.|Cross-zone load balancing distributes traffic; it does not define a scaling cooldown.|Disabling alarms removes responsiveness and is not a controlled automatic stabilization mechanism.","as tracking", "The source does not name the policy type. This answer applies to simple-scaling cooldown behavior; configure instance warm-up for target tracking/step scaling instead of assuming cooldown governs all policies.")
R(326,"A","resilient","Managed application platforms","Elastic Beanstalk manages supported web application environments, deployment artifacts, load balancing and Auto Scaling.",
"Beanstalk provisions the supported platform environment and deploys application versions; separate environments support blue/green cutovers.|CloudFront distributes/caches HTTP content and does not deploy/manage a LAMP server environment.|CodePipeline orchestrates delivery stages but needs a deployment/hosting target rather than supplying this entire environment itself.|AWS DevOps is a practice/category, not the specific managed hosting service described.","beanstalk")
R(327,"C","resilient","Change-driven processing","DynamoDB Streams exposes item-level changes with per-item ordering, allowing checkpointed consumers to process deltas rather than scan the base table.",
"A full table scan reads unchanged items and is the explicit inefficient pattern to avoid.|A second table needs its own reliable change producer; creating it does not automatically capture edits.|Streams supplies insert/update/delete records; consume and checkpoint them within stream retention.|Copying selected changes to S3 requires a change-detection/delivery mechanism that the option does not specify.","dynamostream", "Streams retains records for a bounded window (24 hours), and ordering is per item/shard lineage, not one total global order. Consumers must handle retries idempotently.")
R(328,"B","performance","Database configuration","Change a supported engine parameter through a custom RDS DB parameter group; default groups cannot be edited.",
"Standard RDS does not provide SSH/host configuration-file administration.|Create a compatible custom parameter group, set max_connections and associate/apply it according to the parameter's dynamic/static behavior.|Option groups control supported engine extensions/options, not arbitrary MySQL parameter values such as max_connections.|Default parameter groups cannot be modified; use a custom group.","rdsparameter", "More connections consume memory and can worsen load. Validate instance headroom and use connection pooling/proxy; check whether applying the group/change requires a reboot.")
R(329,"A","cost","Private service access","For VPC-local S3 access, a gateway endpoint avoids NAT processing and interface-endpoint hourly charges.",
"An S3 gateway endpoint provides private routed access and has no additional endpoint hourly/data-processing charge.|An interface endpoint can serve supported private access cases but adds charges and is unnecessary for this VPC-only S3 pattern.|NAT reaches the public service path and adds hourly/processing charges.|An Internet Gateway does not provide the requested private service endpoint and private instances also lack direct public addressing.","s3endpoint")
FIXES[330] = {"option": {1: "Yes. S3 provides strong read-after-write and LIST consistency automatically."}}
R(330,"B","resilient","Object storage consistency","Successful S3 object writes are strongly consistent for subsequent GET and LIST requests against that bucket.",
"The old eventual-consistency assumption for object reads/listing is outdated.|S3 reflects successful object writes in subsequent reads and list operations without an application propagation wait.|S3 is widely used for big-data object storage and processing; the workload category alone does not disqualify it.|No cost inputs establish this blanket claim, and storage cost is not an inconsistency barrier to immediate reads.","consistency", "This guarantee is not synchronous cross-Region replication or invalidation of CDN/browser caches; those have separate behavior.")
