R(419,"B","performance","Incremental ETL","Glue job bookmarks track supported source-processing state so recurring jobs can avoid reprocessing old input.",
"More parallel compute can mask wasted work but does not track which files were processed.|Enable job bookmarks for supported sources/transform contexts and persist job state correctly.|Increasing the dataset makes repeated unnecessary processing worse.|Deleting old inputs through custom Lambda adds code and can violate retention needs when bookmarks suffice.","bookmarks", "Bookmarks depend on supported source semantics and stable transformation contexts; resetting them can deliberately reprocess data.")
R(420,"C","resilient","Hybrid file access","S3 File Gateway presents supported NFS/SMB file shares backed by S3 objects with local caching.",
"An offline device is not an ongoing low-latency standard file-protocol interface.|Volume Gateway exposes block volumes; its stored data is not ordinary file objects directly browsed through S3 APIs.|File Gateway supplies the file-protocol interface and S3-backed durable object representation.|An EC2-attached block volume alone does not provide the managed on-premises file-to-object gateway.","gateway")
R(421,"A","performance","Database read scaling","Aurora's reader endpoint balances connections to replicas, not individual queries perfectly equally.",
"Use the reader endpoint and appropriately distributed/recycled connections to reach the replicas.|The cluster writer endpoint directs connections to the primary writer.|Parallel Query accelerates some analytical work but does not distribute client connections across read replicas.|A custom NLB is unnecessary and does not replace Aurora's supported replica endpoint semantics.","aurora", "The source's 'equally' is an overstatement: connection pooling and query differences can cause unequal load even with endpoint connection balancing.")
R(422,"A","resilient","Managed container migration","EKS retains Kubernetes APIs and DocumentDB is the MongoDB-compatible database option among the listed choices.",
"EKS reduces control-plane management and DocumentDB is the closest listed document-API migration target.|ECS is not Kubernetes and Aurora changes the MongoDB application data API.|EKS Anywhere retains customer-managed on-premises operations and DynamoDB changes the database access model.|ECS changes orchestration and Neptune is a graph database, requiring application changes.","eks documentdb", "A literal no-code-change guarantee cannot be made for arbitrary MongoDB features. Validate compatibility, operators and drivers before migration.")
R(423,"D","cost","Private service access","An S3 gateway endpoint supplies VPC-local private access without NAT or interface endpoint processing/hourly costs.",
"Transit Gateway is a network routing hub, not itself a private S3 service endpoint.|An interface endpoint can work but adds endpoint charges for this VPC-local use case.|A NAT gateway adds egress cost and uses the public service endpoint path.|A gateway endpoint is the low-cost native private route to same-Region S3 from the VPC.","s3endpoint")
R(424,"A","performance","Compute quotas","EC2 On-Demand limits are generally regional vCPU-based quotas by category, not a universal twenty-instance cap per AZ.",
"A regional category vCPU quota can exhaust after a particular number of launches; inspect the actual error and request an increase.|Blind retries do not fix a persistent account quota rejection.|There is no universal twenty-instance-per-AZ launch limit of the stated kind.|The old blanket twenty-instance region limit is not the modern vCPU-category quota model.","as", "Capacity shortages, API throttling and quotas produce different errors. Twenty successes alone does not prove which occurred; the answer is a plausible cause, as asked.")
R(425,"B","performance","Large object uploads","Multipart upload supports large objects and independently retryable parts; a single PUT cannot upload a terabyte object.",
"A single PUT request has a much smaller size limit than one terabyte.|Multipart upload divides the object into supported parts and completes them into one S3 object.|Import/Export is a historical bulk-transfer service, not the interactive application upload API.|Shipping Snowball devices is not a per-user web-upload mechanism and has current availability constraints.","s3limits presign")
R(426,"CD","performance","Database read scaling","Traditional RDS read replicas asynchronously offload reads; a Multi-AZ DB instance standby is for availability.",
"A standard read replica does not accept arbitrary application writes as a co-writer.|A replica does not increase the primary volume's IOPS through Global Accelerator.|Additional replicas add independent read capacity beyond one primary instance.|Asynchronous replicas serve suitable read workloads away from the primary.|Synchronous standby/failover describes the traditional Multi-AZ availability feature rather than the stated read-replica advantage.","rds", "Do not confuse traditional Multi-AZ DB instances with supported Multi-AZ DB clusters that include readable instances.")
R(427,"DE","resilient","Subnet fundamentals","A subnet belongs to one AZ and uses the VPC main route table by default unless explicitly associated with another table.",
"The stated /27 lower bound is wrong; ordinary IPv4 subnets support /28 as the minimum size.|A subnet does not span two Availability Zones.|Private instances can use NAT/other egress without an instance Elastic IP.|A new subnet without an explicit custom association uses the VPC main route table.|Each subnet is created in a single Availability Zone.","subnet vpc")
