# Worksheets 6–7.
R(550,"DE","resilient","Fast database environments","Aurora cloning shares unchanged storage pages through copy-on-write while replicas supply the supported HA compute configuration.",
"A full logical dump repeats the long export/import and production-load problem.|Traditional Multi-AZ adds availability but not the fast Aurora copy-on-write clone feature.|Snapshot/restore can create a staging copy but needs a separate full restoration workflow.|Aurora with suitable multi-AZ readers supplies the resilient compatible database architecture.|Aurora cloning creates a separate writable staging database initially sharing unchanged storage pages.","clone auroraha", "Clones are not backups or automatic production synchronization. Account for changes, engine compatibility and ongoing storage/compute costs.")
R(551,"A","resilient","Tier decoupling","Separate static objects, scalable application workers and a managed multi-AZ relational database.",
"S3 serves static assets, ECS runs independently scaled application tasks, and Multi-AZ RDS protects the relational database.|CloudFront needs an origin and does not itself store the static application; the choice incompletely separates the tiers.|One larger shared static/application host retains coupling and a failure bottleneck.|Moving long-running logic to Lambda may exceed its timeout, and DynamoDB is not a minimal-change MySQL replacement.","s3 fargate rds", "Configure at least two healthy app tasks across failure domains; orchestration alone does not make a one-task service highly available.")
R(552,"B","performance","High-performance file storage","FSx for Lustre supplies a managed parallel POSIX interface with native S3 repository integration.",
"FSx for Windows targets Windows/SMB semantics rather than Lustre HPC processing.|Lustre provides parallel file throughput and supported S3 import/export linkage.|EBS supplies AZ-scoped block devices, not the required managed shared parallel filesystem.|EFS supplies elastic NFS, but Lustre is the specialized high-performance parallel/S3 integration match.","lustre")
R(553,"B","resilient","Container orchestration","ECS schedules container tasks across cluster capacity and integrates with load balancing, monitoring and service scaling.",
"Compute Optimizer recommends resource sizing; it does not orchestrate deployments.|ECS supplies the container placement and service orchestration feature set asked for.|CloudFormation can declare infrastructure but is not the running container scheduler.|Beanstalk manages application environments, but ECS directly controls the task placement across a container cluster described here.","ecs fargate", "Service autoscaling/load-balancer/monitoring integrations still need configuration; ECS does not infer every workload's scaling policy.")
R(554,"C","secure","In-memory authentication","Redis OSS AUTH requires the configured authentication token and encrypted transit on supported ElastiCache configurations.",
"TLS protects network transport but does not by itself require an AUTH password.|At-rest encryption protects stored data and is separate from command authentication.|Configure the supported auth-token and transit-encryption settings to require authentication before Redis commands.|Password authentication is not universally enabled by default for all cache configurations.","redisAuth", "Modern user groups/RBAC can provide finer authorization. Verify engine/version/token update semantics rather than exposing tokens in shell history.")
R(555,"B","performance","Shared container storage","Among these fixed legacy choices, provisioned EFS throughput decouples sustained capacity from stored byte count for a highly concurrent filesystem workload.",
"A File Gateway SMB layer is not the native shared Linux task filesystem configuration requested.|Provisioned throughput supplies configured capacity independently of storage size; the listed Max I/O mode is for high concurrency.|DynamoDB is an API database, not a filesystem mount point.|Bursting ties baseline/credits to stored size and may not sustain a high fixed workload when credits are depleted.","efsperformance", "Do not generalize that Max I/O is always fastest: it adds latency and AWS generally recommends General Purpose/Elastic Throughput for many new workloads. Benchmark actual IOPS/throughput and choose a currently supported combination.")
h(556,"Macie in C is the appropriate PII discovery service, but it does not automatically delete detected objects. Every offered choice omits the required removal/quarantine workflow and approval/retention behavior. A complete design needs findings-driven remediation as well as access policies.","secure","Sensitive data remediation","macie")
R(558,"B","performance","Static global ingress","Global Accelerator supplies stable anycast ingress addresses while regional endpoint groups target supported ALBs.",
"Arbitrary private EC2 addresses are not registered as standard-accelerator endpoints this way.|Use a regional endpoint group per intended Region and register the regional ALB endpoints.|An NLB is regional, and this is not a global NLB fronting all Regions' ALBs.|Periodically changing firewall IP lists adds custom polling and a window of stale addresses.","ga")
FIXES[559] = {"prompt": "A CloudFormation template contains AWSTemplateFormatVersion='2010-09-09', a Parameters section for VPCId/SubnetId, and an Outputs section referring to manilaInstance, but no Resources section. Why will stack creation fail?"}
R(559,"A","resilient","Infrastructure templates","Resources is the required CloudFormation template section; Parameters and Conditions have different optional roles.",
"Without a Resources section, no required resource definitions exist and the output reference cannot resolve.|Parameters is a valid template section and is not inherently a deployment error.|Conditions is optional when conditional resource logic is not needed.|2010-09-09 is the documented template-format version, not the date asserted in this distractor.","cfnfull", "The JSON screenshot was visually checked to restore the omitted template structure instead of inferring it from damaged OCR.")
R(560,"D","performance","Cache freshness","A zero freshness directive can require origin revalidation/fetches unless overridden by the configured cache policy minimum TTL.",
"Previously unseen objects explain a first miss, not repeated origin hits for popular cached content.|An origin group has a primary/secondary; two primaries are not the stated cache-control cause.|Very large objects can have separate caching constraints, but no sizes establish that condition here.|max-age=0 is a plausible repeated-revalidation cause when cache-policy minimum TTL does not override it.","cfinvalidate", "Inspect Cache-Control, minimum/default/maximum TTL and cache-key variance. A positive minimum TTL can override some origin freshness directives.")
R(561,"AE","cost","Decommissioning retained data","Keep required recovery artifacts, remove unneeded running compute, and consider transfer of eligible reservation commitments.",
"Validated EBS snapshots retain the required audit data before compute is terminated.|A Reserved Instance is a billing product; the existing running host is not converted through this step.|Stopping preserves EBS data but leaves ongoing storage and reservation obligations; termination after snapshots is the cleaner decommissioning path.|Changing to Spot does not cancel an existing RI commitment or preserve the audit data automatically.|Eligible Standard RI commitments can be listed in the marketplace; physical EC2 hosts are not sold.","ri ebs", "Confirm retention and successful recovery before deletion. Marketplace sale is eligibility/buyer dependent and does not immediately guarantee an end to charges.")
R(562,"C","resilient","Queue-driven scaling","A durable SQS backlog decouples services while Auto Scaling adjusts worker capacity to pending work.",
"Step Functions orchestrates states; it is not the general durable message-buffer queue described.|An SNS topic is not a competing-consumer work queue with the required retention/visibility behavior.|SQS provides buffering, and a backlog-aware ASG policy adds/removes worker capacity.|A fixed worker fleet can consume SQS but lacks the demand-adjusted capacity and cost behavior requested.","sqs as")
FIXES[563] = {"option": {3: "Use a Transit Gateway in each Region with VPC attachments and inter-Region peering. Route the relevant traffic symmetrically through AWS Network Firewall endpoints using the appropriate inspection topology."}}
R(563,"D","secure","Network inspection","Cross-Region VPC routing needs regional gateways/peering and explicit inspection routes; a firewall is not implied by mere connectivity.",
"Route 53 traffic policies do not connect private VPC networks, and DNS Firewall does not inspect all IP traffic.|Direct Connect gateways are for hybrid connectivity and Security Hub aggregates findings rather than filtering packets.|NAT and Session Manager do not create the requested transitive inter-VPC IPS routing topology.|Regional TGWs with peering provide connectivity and Network Firewall supplies configured stateful inspection along the routed paths.","networkfirewall dxgateway", "Editorial correction: the source suggests a single TGW for VPC attachments in different Regions; it now explicitly uses regional TGWs, peering and symmetric inspection routes.")
R(564,"C","performance","Analytical storage formats","Columnar Parquet reduces unnecessary scans for suitable queries; Lake Formation and IAM permissions must both authorize the data access workflow.",
"Compression alone does not supply column pruning, and generic confused-deputy conditions are not the complete Lake Formation authorization model.|CSV remains row-oriented and lacks Parquet's columnar read advantages.|Parquet enables columnar query efficiency; grant the required IAM GetDataAccess and actual Lake Formation/table/column permissions.|Minification only strips whitespace and does not create efficient typed columnar storage.","athena lakepermissions", "lakeformation:GetDataAccess alone does not grant a user arbitrary table access. Retain Lake Formation grants, encryption authorization and relevant location permissions.")
R(565,"C","resilient","Object replication prerequisites","S3 replication requires versioning on both source and destination buckets.",
"Replication is not restricted to an Enterprise support-plan feature.|It is not limited to objects in Standard-IA.|Enable the required bucket versioning and replication permissions/configuration.|One Zone-IA is not the only storage class eligible for replication.","crr")
R(566,"A","performance","Global DNS routing","Route 53 latency routing chooses among regional DNS records; ALBs/NLBs are regional traffic-distribution resources.",
"Configure latency records for the eligible regional endpoints and the desired public name.|One ALB does not span all the stated Regions.|One NLB is also regional rather than the requested DNS latency selection mechanism.|DataSync transfers data and does not route viewer DNS requests.","routing")
R(567,"D","secure","Workload credentials","Remove embedded credentials from images and use an instance role for temporary AWS API access.",
"Moving the same long-term secret to S3 retains a bootstrapping/access-key problem.|An AMI is not a secure secret vault and copies propagate exposed credentials.|Glacier is not a runtime credential service and does not solve long-term key exposure.|A scoped role eliminates the need for the application to carry static embedded access keys.","iam", "Revoke/rotate exposed keys as part of the actual remediation; deleting them from one AMI does not invalidate existing copies.")
R(568,"C","secure","IAM user API access","Creating an IAM user does not automatically create API credentials or attach authorization policies.",
"MFA strengthens authentication but alone creates neither API keys nor service permissions.|Permissions alone do not give this newly created user an API signing credential.|For the specific long-term IAM-user flow, create programmatic credentials and grant only required actions/resources.|A new user has no automatic access-key pair or general permission to call protected resource APIs.","iam", "Prefer federation/role-based temporary credentials for real applications when possible; this question specifically asks about a newly created IAM user.")
R(569,"D","performance","Multi-consumer streams","Kinesis Data Streams retains telemetry for independent consumers and near-real-time processing.",
"AppStream streams applications to users rather than ingesting vehicle telemetry.|EMR processes data but is not the live multi-consumer stream buffer itself.|Consumers of one SQS queue compete for work instead of each reading a retained full stream.|Data Streams supplies retained records and independent consuming applications for the tracking/aggregation flow.","kinesis")
R(570,"B","resilient","Disaster recovery","DRS continuous replication with minimal staging resources is a lower-cost recovery pattern than keeping a full active duplicate.",
"An active-active licensed enterprise database/application deployment has significantly more standing capacity and complexity.|DRS continuously replicates supported servers and launches recovery resources from a small staging footprint.|Periodic backups alone do not establish the specified sixty-second data-loss objective.|A warm RDS/DMS migration requires application/database compatibility and additional running resources versus the offered server-recovery pattern.","drs dr", "Validate licensing, supported OS/storage, replication lag and full recovery through drills. RPO/RTO are workload objectives, not unconditional DRS guarantees.")
R(571,"C","performance","Large object transfers","Multipart upload parallelizes large object uploads and retries individual failed parts.",
"A faster network adapter may help some bottlenecks but is not the native large-object upload protocol optimization.|Provisioned local/block I/O is not by itself an S3 transfer parallelization mechanism.|Multipart upload is the recommended large-object transfer pattern for independent parts.|CloudFront POST forwarding does not inherently provide multipart retry/parallelism or solve this EC2-to-S3 path.","s3limits")
R(572,"BD","secure","Certificate expiry monitoring","Use ACM expiry events or a valid Config expiry evaluation with an event/notification integration.",
"The asserted Trusted Advisor-to-OpsItem metric path is not the direct ACM expiry-notification solution.|Match supported ACM near-expiry events/thresholds in EventBridge and notify an SNS topic.|Replacing public website certificates with a private CA breaks ordinary public trust and is unnecessary for notifications.|A supported managed/custom Config expiry rule can produce compliance changes that EventBridge routes to SNS.|A scheduled EventBridge rule does not itself query CloudWatch metrics; an alarm or compute/API target is missing.","acmevents config", "ACM has supported near-expiry events and Config managed rule options. Configure the day threshold and test delivery rather than assuming all events fire only on day thirty.")
R(573,"A","performance","HTTP content routing","ALB path-pattern conditions route /api/android and /api/ios to their respective target groups.",
"Path conditions inspect the request URL path and select the configured target group.|Host rules inspect the hostname rather than the Android/iOS path segment.|NLB does not support ALB-style HTTP URL content rules.|GWLB is for network appliances rather than application path routing.","alb")
R(574,"CE","secure","Database transport encryption","Force TLS server-side and configure the client to trust/verify the RDS certificate; TDE protects a different stage.",
"Transparent Data Encryption protects stored database data, not the client-to-server transport.|IAM database authentication is not the SQL Server TLS configuration requested.|The supported rds.force_ssl parameter requires encrypted client connections after the needed apply/reboot operation.|SQL Server traffic does not become HTTPS merely by allowing TCP 443.|Install the appropriate CA trust and configure TLS with hostname/certificate verification in the database client.","rdstls", "Use a custom parameter group and current RDS CA bundle/client settings; preserve the actual database port in network controls.")
h(575,"The original PNG shows one entire IAM policy option only; the remaining candidate policies are not in the upload. The visible policy correctly scopes GetItem/PutItem/UpdateItem/DeleteItem to one table ARN, but inventing three unseen alternatives would not reconstruct the source question.","secure","IAM policy scope","iam dynamo", "Item CRUD permissions should be resource-scoped to the table ARN. Query/Scan/batch/index actions are separate permissions and are not implied by these four actions.")
R(576,"A","resilient","Function execution limits","A Lambda invocation cannot execute beyond the configured timeout or the service maximum of fifteen minutes.",
"Jobs averaging fifteen minutes can exceed the maximum on slower invocations and be terminated.|An internal service error is possible generally, but the stated near-maximum duration points more directly to timeout.|Recursion is not established by the question; exceeding duration does not prove recursive code.|Concurrency exhaustion generally throttles admission rather than explaining an already running long invocation being timed out.","lambda", "Use checkpointed/idempotent tasks, split work or move long jobs to appropriate Batch/container/workflow compute. Retries do not make a single invocation's duration unlimited.")
R(577,"A","performance","Scaling fundamentals","Vertical scaling adds resources to a node; horizontal scaling adds nodes and requires a design that distributes state/work.",
"Larger individual machines are scale-up; adding machines is scale-out.|Serverless is a compute operating model, not the definition of vertical scaling.|This reverses the standard horizontal and vertical definitions.|Container size is not the definition of horizontal scaling, and adding machines is not vertical scaling.","as", "Horizontal systems still face coordination, partitioning and downstream bottlenecks; the source's 'doesn't run into limitations' is not a promise of unlimited scale.")
R(578,"B","secure","Database authentication","Supported RDS IAM database authentication uses short-lived signed tokens derived from the workload role rather than a stored database password.",
"Aurora Backtrack rewinds supported database state, not workload-role authentication.|Enable supported IAM DB auth, map the database user and authorize the instance role to connect.|A CA parameter configures transport trust but does not replace password authentication by itself.|At-rest encryption does not authorize database users or configure client TLS.","iamdb", "Use a supported engine/version, a TLS-verified connection and token generation from temporary role credentials; IAM authorization does not automatically grant SQL-level privileges.")
R(579,"C","cost","Managed API hosting","API Gateway and Lambda provide demand-driven HTTP APIs without retaining a dedicated application-host fleet.",
"S3 static hosting cannot execute arbitrary REST API server logic.|Spot/EFA adds host management and interruption exposure; EFA is not a general public REST API accelerator.|API Gateway supplies managed API ingress and Lambda runs suitable bounded stateless request handlers on demand.|Fargate is a valid container option but retains task sizing/running capacity; the serverless API pair is the direct fit for suitable short request handlers.","apigw lambda", "Actual cost depends on duration, volume and constraints; Lambda is not universally cheaper and existing APIs may need adaptation.")
R(580,"D","performance","Transport load balancing","NLB is the managed Layer-4 TCP/TLS/UDP load balancer, including supported IP targets for Fargate tasks.",
"Weighted DNS selects endpoints but is not a per-connection Layer-4 load balancer.|Fargate supports ALB/NLB integration; a custom load-balancer task is unnecessary.|ALB handles HTTP-layer routing rather than arbitrary raw TCP gaming protocols.|NLB supplies high-throughput transport-level connection distribution to the supported targets.","nlb fargate", "NLB is regional; global users may need an additional regional/global routing design depending on latency/failover requirements.")
h(581,"D can scale ECS service desired task count on memory. C/E instead say to scale an ECS cluster using a service/target-group CPU metric, without an ASG capacity-provider or matching task-count policy; ALBs have no selectable host CPU metric as in B. The options do not provide two complete native service-scaling policies.","performance","Container scaling","fargate as")
R(582,"B","secure","Managed file transfer","Transfer Family VPC-hosted internet-facing endpoints allow SG source filtering and supported EFS-backed SFTP access.",
"An EC2 SFTP server is not the managed serverless service and S3 is not an attached block volume.|Use a public-facing VPC-hosted Transfer endpoint, restrict its SG to approved sources and configure EFS user access.|Transfer Family does not attach an EBS volume as its managed server storage backend.|A service PUBLIC endpoint does not offer the same customer SG filtering as the specified VPC-hosted endpoint.","transfer efs", "Configure the supported identity provider, POSIX user/path permissions, mount targets and capacity/performance requirements; 'high IOPS' still needs sizing.")
R(583,"D","performance","Elastic application tiers","Fargate removes container-host management and Aurora Serverless v2 scales relational compute for variable OLTP demand.",
"Beanstalk with provisioned RDS can work but does not supply the listed serverless database compute elasticity.|ASG and provisioned RDS retain baseline sizing/host considerations.|Redshift is an analytical warehouse, not the transactional relational database replacement here.|Fargate tasks and Aurora Serverless v2 provide the closest managed elastic application/OLTP combination among the choices.","fargate serverless", "Configure task autoscaling/minimums and supported database capacity settings. Idle tasks still cost money and pause-to-zero/resume behavior depends on engine/version/settings and latency requirements.")
R(584,"D","cost","Shared network allowlists","A RAM-shared customer-managed prefix list centralizes reusable CIDRs referenced in supported security-group rules.",
"ARC routing controls govern recovery traffic state, not shared SG CIDR list administration.|Firewall Manager can enforce broader SG policy but is more machinery/cost than sharing a simple reused CIDR set.|Customers edit customer-managed lists, not AWS-managed service lists; Security Hub is not the described prefix-list policy executor.|Manage the CIDR list once, share it through RAM and reference it in the appropriate regional security-group rules.","prefix", "Prefix lists are regional and consume rule quota by maximum entries. SG rules are allow-only; removing an allow CIDR is not an explicit deny override.")
MERGES[585] = [586]
FIXES[585] = {"prompt": "A company that is not an existing CloudTrail Lake customer retains multi-account CloudTrail trails in S3. It needs a managed SQL query solution for AccessDenied/Unauthorized errors. Which listed solution is appropriate under current service availability?"}
R(585,"C","secure","Audit log analytics","Athena can query S3 trail logs; CloudTrail Lake new-customer eligibility is a separate current service constraint.",
"CloudTrail Lake closed to new customers in May 2026, so this explicitly new customer cannot choose it.|Security Hub findings are not a raw CloudTrail SQL log-query engine.|Catalog/partition the retained S3 trail logs and use Athena SQL filters on errorCode and related fields.|A visualization layer alone does not parse/query the raw retained log objects with the least additional machinery.","athena cloudtrail", "Editorial update explicitly identifies a new Lake customer and S3-retained trails. For an eligible existing Lake customer, its SQL query feature is a different valid operational option.")
R(587,"B","performance","Durable parallel filesystems","Persistent Lustre is the managed parallel filesystem deployment for retained HPC working data; scratch sacrifices durability for temporary processing.",
"File Cache is not the persistent parallel storage service asked for and has service availability constraints.|Persistent FSx for Lustre supplies parallel storage with retained data and failure recovery properties appropriate to this comparison.|Scratch is intended for replaceable temporary data and does not replicate files for the same persistent guarantees.|EFS is elastic shared NFS but not the specialized parallel Lustre HPC filesystem requested.","lustre", "Persistent Lustre remains an AZ-scoped deployment; use S3 repository/backup/DR strategies where AZ-loss protection is required.")
R(588,"B","resilient","Managed message brokers","A RabbitMQ cluster deployment, multi-AZ app capacity and RDS Multi-AZ preserve interfaces while reducing host/broker/database operations.",
"An active/standby pair is the ActiveMQ pattern, not the RabbitMQ clustered deployment specified, and cross-Region readers are not the same managed Multi-AZ failover.|A supported RabbitMQ cluster plus multi-AZ consumers and Multi-AZ PostgreSQL addresses each current single-AZ component.|OpenSearch is not a general replacement runtime for the consumer application and SQS changes the broker interface.|Self-managed RabbitMQ ASGs retain broker operations; Aurora cloning does not migrate the original database or create ongoing HA.","mq rds", "Configure durable queues/quorum behavior, health checks and enough consumer capacity; simply naming multiple AZs does not replicate every application state.")
FIXES[589] = {"prompt": "A public ALB must distribute traffic to a private application tier. A separate private database tier must not share its subnets/route tables with other tiers. Each tier must span at least two AZs for availability. What is the minimum subnet count?", "options": ["3", "4", "2", "6"]}
R(589,"D","resilient","Multi-tier network design","Three isolated tiers across two AZs require two public ALB subnets, two private application subnets and two private database subnets.",
"Three subnets provide only one per tier and do not place every tier in two AZs.|Four subnets cannot provide three separate two-AZ tiers under these isolation requirements.|Two subnets cannot separate all three tiers and supply their multi-AZ placement.|Six subnets provide the three isolated tier types in each of two AZs.","subnet alb", "The original four numeric options were restored visually and the minimum two-AZ assumption made explicit. Security groups/routes must still enforce tier-to-tier access.")
R(590,"C","resilient","Container observability","Container Insights provides managed collection/visualization for supported Kubernetes/container metrics with configured application log collection.",
"Prometheus collects metrics, while the proposal does not supply the corresponding application log pipeline.|OpenSearch can serve an observability design but needs more log/metric shipping and domain configuration for this use case.|Install/configure the CloudWatch observability/Container Insights components and supported log collector for the cluster.|An agent collecting only metrics does not automatically supply all application logs or the container-specific insight views.","containerinsights", "Collectors/permissions must be installed/configured; enabling a dashboard does not retroactively collect every application's logs.")
FIXES[591] = {"prompt": "Two EC2 instances must run continuously for three years with stable predictable utilization. Which listed purchasing option best reduces cost for this non-interruptible baseline?", "options": ["Spot instances", "Reserved Instances", "On-Demand instances", "Dedicated Hosts"]}
R(591,"B","cost","Steady compute purchasing","A well-utilized reservation commitment discounts a predictable non-interruptible baseline.",
"Spot is interruptible and conflicts with the continuous baseline requirement.|A matching three-year reservation discounts steady usage compared with On-Demand.|On-Demand avoids commitment but misses the offered long-term utilization discount.|Dedicated Hosts target tenancy/licensing rather than a generic steady usage discount.","ri savings", "All four choices were restored from the screenshot. A billing commitment does not provide application HA, and reservation attributes/payment options affect price.")
R(592,"D","resilient","Database point-in-time recovery","DynamoDB PITR restores a new table to a selected available point before unwanted edits.",
"Global replication also replicates mistakes and is not historical point-in-time recovery.|A custom stream replay/undo system needs retained state and operations that managed PITR avoids.|Daily exports do not guarantee recovery to an arbitrary hour-before-incident timestamp.|Enable PITR beforehand and restore to an available timestamp before the accidental changes.","dynamopitr", "Restore creates another table and needs an application cutover plan. Retention/settings and latest restorable time limit available recovery points.")
h(593,"The source requires Apache Ranger integration without selecting an EMR release, but native Ranger integration is unsupported starting with EMR 7.4. Simultaneous Lake Formation authorization also has mode/release restrictions. The steady-core/Spot-task cost pattern alone cannot satisfy this unspecified current feature combination.","secure","Big data authorization","ranger lakepermissions")
R(594,"A","secure","Key rotation control","A customer-managed KMS key exposes supported rotation controls; AWS-managed/owned keys do not give the customer the same schedule/configuration authority.",
"Use a supported customer-managed symmetric key and configure the desired rotation policy.|AWS controls AWS-managed key rotation; the customer cannot set its policy like a customer-managed key.|AWS-owned keys are not customer-provisioned keys with customer rotation policy controls.|Imported external material requires a different material lifecycle and adds overhead for the stated goal.","kmsrotation ebs", "Existing unencrypted volumes require an encrypted copy/snapshot/new-volume migration; key rotation does not encrypt existing plaintext or rewrite existing ciphertext.")
h(595,"Snowball is the historical offline-transfer answer, but it is not available to new customers after the November 2025 availability change. The source gives no existing-customer eligibility or target deadline; Transfer Acceleration cannot repair a slow local uplink. A current eligible transfer plan cannot be selected unconditionally.","performance","Bulk migration","datasync transferaccel")
R(596,"ABE","cost","Scale-in selection","The default policy favors AZ balance, then obsolete launch configuration/template lineage, then applicable billing-time tie-breaks.",
"AZ balancing first considers the most populated eligible AZ, us-west-1a here.|For remaining ties the documented billing-hour proximity rule can apply.|The policy does not generally prefer deleting the newest desired launch-template generation first.|Removing from the least populated AZ worsens balance.|Oldest/outdated launch-template generations are eligible priority candidates in the documented hierarchy.|Farthest from the billing hour is not the stated tie-break preference.","terminationpolicy", "Mixed purchase options, version/default changes, instance weights and per-second billing can alter practical effects. Follow the current documented hierarchy rather than treating age alone as the full algorithm.")
R(597,"A","secure","API auditing","CloudTrail records supported Redshift management API actions for audit; query execution auditing is a separate requirement.",
"CloudTrail supplies identity/time/request context for supported AWS API operations.|X-Ray traces instrumented application requests, not the authoritative management audit record.|CloudWatch metrics/logs do not automatically record every API caller identity.|Spectrum runs external S3 queries; it is not an API audit logging feature.","audit", "Enable the proper event categories/retention. SQL statement/activity auditing requires the relevant Redshift audit/database logging configuration too.")
R(598,"B","resilient","Quota awareness","Trusted Advisor recommendations include supported service-limit/usage checks, distinct from vulnerability or spend analysis.",
"Inspector assesses software vulnerabilities/exposure rather than generic account service quota usage.|Trusted Advisor supplies supported best-practice/service-limit checks and recommended actions.|Cost Explorer analyzes costs, not the primary quota recommendation inventory.|Budgets watches spend/usage thresholds rather than every service's capacity quota.","advisor", "Coverage and refresh/access vary by support/service. Use Service Quotas metrics/alarms and actual error handling where exact quota control is required.")
FIXES[599] = {"option": {4: "Create an AWS WAF web ACL and associate it with the internet-facing ALB for the public application."}}
R(599,"DE","secure","Protected HTTP ingress","ALB supports HTTP-layer sticky sessions and direct WAF web ACL association.",
"Network Firewall inspects routed traffic but is not the ALB/WAF sticky-session configuration.|An NLB does not provide the ALB HTTP cookie stickiness/WAF association combination asked here.|GWLB targets network appliances rather than web cookie session affinity.|Use an internet-facing ALB and enable the appropriate target-group session stickiness.|Associate the WAF web ACL with the supported ALB resource to filter application requests.","alb waf", "Editorial correction: a WAF ACL is associated with a supported resource, not just 'configured with a public domain'. HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect, TLS and externalized session state should be considered separately.")
R(600,"D","performance","Object metadata architecture","Keep large image bytes in S3 and keyed geographic metadata/pointers in DynamoDB.",
"Storing all large binary images in a relational database adds storage/query management for this simple keyed model.|DynamoDB has an item-size limit, so high-resolution image bodies are not a general fit in the table.|Keyspaces can store keys but adds a Cassandra-compatible access model without a stated compatibility need.|S3 handles image objects and DynamoDB supplies scalable low-latency geographic-key lookups.","dynamo s3", "Design around hot geographic keys, versioned pointers and metadata/object update consistency; this is not automatic GIS spatial indexing.")
R(601,"B","secure","Routed threat inspection","Network Firewall enforces configured stateless/stateful inspection on traffic deliberately routed through its endpoints.",
"WAF filters supported HTTP applications, not every inbound/outbound VPC protocol.|Network Firewall supplies the routed traffic inspection and rule-based blocking capability.|Traffic mirroring copies packets to a sensor; the mirrored sensor cannot automatically block the original inline path.|GuardDuty detects findings and can feed response automation, but is not the inline bidirectional filtering service.","networkfirewall", "Configure endpoints/routes symmetrically and avoid bypass paths. A firewall does not inspect all VPC traffic just because it exists.")
R(603,"A","secure","Customer MFA","Cognito user-pool MFA adds a supported second sign-in factor beyond a password.",
"Configure required/optional supported MFA methods in the user pool and the application challenge flow.|An IAM policy controls AWS authorization, not the user's second authentication challenge.|Custom challenge code can be built but is unnecessary for ordinary supported user-pool MFA.|Manually combining SNS mobile push/SMS does not by itself create the supported Cognito MFA flow.","cognito")
R(604,"D","performance","Serverless business analytics","Glue catalogs S3 data, Athena runs SQL and QuickSight visualizes the results without a dedicated warehouse.",
"An OpenSearch cluster can analyze data but adds a running search/index infrastructure for this periodic SQL-report need.|Streaming the static S3 report dataset through Kinesis adds an unnecessary ingestion/processing path.|Loading Redshift adds warehouse resources not required for the simple periodic analysis.|The catalog/query/dashboard combination supplies managed S3 reporting with low provisioning overhead.","athena quicksight glue")
R(605,"A","resilient","Ephemeral storage lifecycle","Attached instance-store contents do not survive an EBS-backed instance stop/start.",
"The local instance-store device is ephemeral and its earlier contents are lost on stop/start.|EBS is persistent block storage and is not inherently discarded by an ordinary stop.|No malware evidence is supplied; the storage lifecycle explains the result.|Insufficient capacity would cause write errors, not normal stop/start data disappearance.","ec2billing ebs", "An instance-store-backed root instance cannot be stopped the same way. This instance type uses an EBS root with additional local instance-store devices.")
h(606,"Every option confuses ECS task execution role with the application task role, and several invent a standard RDS data VPC endpoint or allow public S3 access. The correct pattern needs a task role plus private RDS SG restrictions and scoped S3 authorization; no listed choice fully provides it.","secure","Container data authorization","taskrole s3endpoint")
h(607,"DataSync can preserve supported SMB permissions using an appropriately deployed agent and identity mapping, but the other proposed paths via ordinary S3 copies discard the required Windows security metadata. Snowcone/Snowmobile options are obsolete/inaccurate and the prompt requires TWO complete solutions. Only one plausible path is offered.","resilient","Windows file migration","datasync fsxwindows")
R(608,"D","cost","Compute right-sizing","Compute Optimizer uses supported utilization evidence to recommend resource changes; observation alone does not supply the same managed sizing assessment.",
"CloudWatch metrics validate the symptom but a dashboard alone does not generate the complete sizing recommendation/action workflow.|Trusted Advisor's cost checks are narrower than the stated multi-resource sizing analysis.|More budget spent is not evidence the chosen instance type fixes the bottleneck.|Enable supported Compute Optimizer analysis, inspect its recommendations and validate appropriate changes.","computeoptimizer cloudwatch", "Recommendations are evidence-based suggestions, not automatic proof CPU is the bottleneck. Check data sufficiency, workload seasonality and the currently supported resource/metric set.")
R(609,"C","resilient","Filesystem replication","ONTAP SnapMirror supplies native volume replication between compatible FSx for ONTAP filesystems, retaining the protocol/data-management model.",
"A custom/agent data-copy pipeline adds orchestration and may not retain all ONTAP replication semantics.|The proposed APIs and direct snapshot-to-S3 workflow are not the native ONTAP DR replication mechanism.|Configure SnapMirror relationships, networking and schedules between the compatible source/destination systems.|A restore-from-backup design can be useful but is not the low-overhead native ongoing volume-replication choice.","snapmirror", "Replication is not an independent immutable backup. Test promotion/resynchronization, directory access, RPO and failback.")
R(610,"D","secure","Encryption compliance remediation","Config identifies supported unencrypted volumes, and Systems Manager automation can coordinate a supported encrypted-copy replacement.",
"A KMS key policy does not by itself prevent creating every unencrypted EBS volume.|Shield does not scan EBS encryption configuration.|Inspector is not the primary managed EBS encryption-configuration evaluator.|IAM/organization creation guardrails plus Config and supported SSM remediation automate prevention/detection/correction.","config ebs ssm", "EBS cannot be encrypted in place. Remediation must snapshot/copy/create/reattach with data consistency, reboot/downtime and rollback controls; a blanket automation statement does not remove these steps.")
h(611,"AWS Proton is historical material with a service availability/end-of-support transition; the prompt does not establish an eligible supported environment. Its components feature is the intended old answer, but not a general current platform recommendation. Use the service notice to select an available deployment platform.","resilient","Platform templates","proton cfnfull")
R(612,"A","secure","Sensitive data discovery","Macie managed/custom identifiers detect sensitive content in supported S3 objects; inventory metadata alone cannot inspect PII.",
"Macie is the managed S3 sensitive-data discovery service for these identifier types.|Audit Manager gathers assessment evidence, not a general object-body content scanner.|DataBrew is data preparation and can help cleansing, but Macie is the direct comprehensive discovery choice.|S3 Inventory describes objects/metadata; it does not expose the contents needed to detect passport or card numbers.","macie", "Detection is not prevention: implement an ingestion quarantine/remediation workflow if discovered data must never reach production lake tables.")
R(613,"B","secure","Governed analytical data","Ingest compatible data into a governed lake and use Lake Formation column filters through Athena to the BI layer.",
"A generic IAM policy on an S3 dataset does not itself enforce query-time column-level table access.|A supported incremental blueprint/ingestion setup plus Lake Formation data filters and Athena provides the stated governed query path.|S3 bucket policies do not enforce SQL column visibility, and the named legacy service does not establish this design.|Filtering one EMR export is not a reusable authorization boundary for all users/queries.","lakepermissions athena quicksight", "Verify blueprint/connector availability and supported Aurora sources. Lake Formation governs the lake copy, not direct SQL access to Aurora; address freshness and use BI column controls as an additional layer where needed.")
R(614,"A","secure","Tier access control","Reference the application security group in the database's inbound database-port rule so ASG instance IP changes do not require rule edits.",
"The SG reference follows the authorized app tier and narrows database ingress.|A subnet NACL affects unrelated resources and the stated rule does not deny other sources by itself.|Fixed IP allowlists become stale as Auto Scaling replaces or adds instances.|Subnet-wide NACL source ranges admit other hosts in that subnet and require explicit stateless return rules.","vpc")
R(615,"C","cost","Data transfer placement","Same-AZ private-IP instance traffic avoids cross-AZ transfer charges for the stated EC2-to-EC2 processing exchange.",
"Private placement in different AZs does not eliminate cross-AZ processing traffic charges.|An ALB adds distribution, not removal of cross-AZ instance-to-instance transfer.|Colocating communicating workers in one AZ with private addressing avoids the cross-AZ charge for that path.|One Region can still contain different AZs, so same-Region placement alone does not remove cross-AZ charges.","ebs vpc", "This is a cost tradeoff, not an HA recommendation. Check S3 endpoint/NAT paths and the actual service-specific transfer pricing separately.")
R(617,"D","resilient","Managed monitoring tools","Managed Prometheus receives metrics and Managed Grafana queries/visualizes supported data sources.",
"Importing a VM retains patching, scaling and availability work for self-hosted monitoring.|The source reverses roles: Grafana is the visualization layer, not the Prometheus metric-storage source described.|Self-hosting both tools on ECS still requires their capacity, persistence and upgrades.|Use a managed Prometheus workspace with configured scraping/remote write and Grafana as a querying dashboard client.","containerinsights", "Configure supported collectors and IAM/data sources. A workspace alone does not automatically discover/ingest every cluster metric.")
R(618,"DE","resilient","Protected cross-Region backups","Use Multi-AZ live storage for local failover and compliance-mode Backup Vault Lock for non-bypassable retained recovery points after its grace period.",
"Governance mode permits authorized lock removal and is weaker than the specified no-deletion requirement.|Custom Lambda backup scheduling adds operations instead of the supported AWS Backup policy mechanism.|Single-AZ storage does not provide the same ready Multi-AZ failover capability for the short RTO.|Multi-AZ FSx plus AWS Backup cross-Region copies combines local availability with regional recovery artifacts.|Compliance-mode Vault Lock, once locked, protects recovery points according to the configured minimum retention.","fsxwindows vaultlock backup", "Protect the destination vault/copies too. Vault retention does not make live files WORM, and a regional backup restore is not guaranteed to complete in ten minutes; test the actual failure scenario.")
R(619,"AC","performance","Dual-stack DNS","Route 53 alias A and AAAA records target the ALB for IPv4 and IPv6 respectively.",
"Alias A directs IPv4 lookups to the load balancer resource.|A CNAME alias is not the correct apex dual-stack record type.|Alias AAAA supplies the dual-stack IPv6 target path.|A fixed ordinary A record loses automatic tracking of ALB address changes.|MX records select mail servers, not a web load-balancer IPv6 endpoint.","dns", "Enable a supported dual-stack ALB configuration and security/routing for IPv6; record creation alone does not enable the network path.")
h(620,"A cleans snapshots and manages future snapshot lifecycle, but none of the options addresses removal/replacement of the explicitly unused EBS volumes. EBS Elastic Volumes cannot shrink a volume in place, so B/C are invalid. A complete cost fix must also identify safely deletable volumes or migrate to smaller ones.","cost","Block storage costs","dlm ebs")
R(621,"B","secure","Log integrity","Enable CloudTrail signed digest/log-file validation to detect changes independently of at-rest encryption.",
"Insights detects unusual API activity patterns, not log-file cryptographic integrity.|Log file integrity validation provides signed digest evidence for validating delivered logs.|CloudWatch monitoring alone does not create the missing signed digest proof.|A log processing library reads/processes files but does not retrospectively create cryptographic validation digests.","trailvalidation", "Enable on the relevant trails and actually validate retained log/digest files. It does not retroactively generate digests for earlier unvalidated delivery.")
