FIXES[581] = {"option": {2: "Configure ECS service desired-task-count autoscaling based on the service's CPU utilization."}}
R(581,"CD","performance","Container service scaling","Scale service task count on supported utilization metrics; host capacity may additionally need a capacity provider/ASG strategy.",
"An unreachable ALB can signal many failures and does not by itself define a useful service capacity metric.|An ALB does not expose customer host CPU utilization as the claimed metric.|ECS service CPU utilization can drive supported desired-task-count autoscaling.|ECS service memory utilization can drive supported desired-task-count autoscaling.|An ALB target group does not expose the generic group CPU metric/cluster scaling mechanism asserted.","fargate as", "Editorial correction changes 'scale the cluster' to the actual ECS service task-count target. For EC2-backed services, separately ensure enough host capacity and drain/scale-in behavior.")
FIXES[620] = {"option": {0: "Safely delete confirmed unused EBS volumes and unneeded snapshots after validating retention/recovery. Use DLM to manage creation and retention of future snapshots."}}
R(620,"A","cost","Block storage cleanup","Remove unused billable resources and automate future snapshot retention; Elastic Volumes does not shrink existing volumes.",
"This addresses both unused resource types and adds a native managed future snapshot lifecycle.|Observing logs does not reclaim unused resources, and in-place EBS shrink is unsupported.|A custom deployment/script cannot make an unsupported in-place volume shrink work.|One-off snapshot deletion does not address unused volumes or future retention growth.","dlm ebs", "Editorial correction adds the unused-volume cleanup missing from the source's best option. Shrinking needed storage requires a validated new-volume/data migration, not an Elastic Volumes resize-down call.")
FIXES[722] = {"options": ["Require aws:kms headers and use Object Lock to rotate the key.", "Require AES256 headers and rely on SSE-S3 to guarantee an annual configurable key rotation schedule.", "Use a supported customer-managed symmetric KMS key, enable automatic 365-day rotation, set bucket SSE-KMS default encryption to that key, and enforce the intended key/encryption policy.", "Attach an SCP directly to the bucket and configure SSE-S3 annual rotation."]}
R(722,"C","secure","Explicit key rotation","A supported customer-managed KMS key exposes an automatic annual rotation policy; S3 defaults and access policies enforce the storage encryption choice.",
"Object Lock enforces retention, not key rotation.|SSE-S3 manages keys internally and does not expose the promised annual customer-configurable rotation schedule.|A compatible customer-managed KMS key can rotate automatically while S3 uses it through the configured SSE-KMS policy.|SCPs do not attach to buckets, and SSE-S3 rotation is not configurable in the asserted way.","kmsrotation sse", "Editorial correction replaces the original manual-rotation C with the required supported automatic rotation configuration and removes OCR-split choices. Rotation does not retroactively rewrite older objects; remediate old data separately.")

